(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["pharmacology-i-exam-3-antiarrhythmic-misc-agents-quiz"]={"title":"Adenosine, Digoxin & Magnesium Sulfate Quiz 1 — Pharmacology I Exam 3","category":"Pharmacology I","exam":"Exam 3","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Outside classes I to IV, which two drugs make up the antiarrhythmic \"others\" group?","choices":["Amiodarone and sotalol","Esmolol and verapamil","Lidocaine and flecainide","Adenosine and digitalis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The classification lists adenosine and digitalis (digoxin) as the others; magnesium sulfate joins them as a miscellaneous agent. — Antiarrhythmics.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"What should a patient be warned about just before receiving adenosine?","choices":["The heart may stop for a few seconds","The skin may turn blue-gray","The ears may ring with dizziness","The hands may shake with blurred vision"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Adenosine may stop the heart for 2 to 3 seconds, and flushing, shortness of breath and chest burning can follow. — Antiarrhythmics.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"Which arrhythmia is the main listed indication for adenosine?","choices":["Paroxysmal supraventricular tachycardia","Refractory ventricular tachycardia","Ventricular fibrillation","Torsades de pointes"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Adenosine is listed for paroxysmal supraventricular tachycardia. — Antiarrhythmics.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"Which adverse effect occurs in about one in five patients given adenosine?","choices":["Nystagmus","Thrombocytopenia","Corneal deposits","Flushing"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Flushing occurs in about 20% of patients given adenosine, with shortness of breath and chest burning also listed. — Antiarrhythmics.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"Which agent is given intravenously for torsades de pointes?","choices":["Dofetilide","Magnesium sulfate","Amiodarone","Procainamide"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Intravenous magnesium sulfate is the listed treatment for torsades de pointes. — Antiarrhythmics.pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"What is digoxin's primary molecular action?","choices":["Potassium channel activation","Sodium-potassium ATPase inhibition","Potassium channel blockade","Beta-1 receptor blockade"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Digoxin (digitalis) inhibits the sodium-potassium ATPase, slows atrioventricular conduction and can induce heart block. — Antiarrhythmics.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"Which drugs are listed to avoid alongside digoxin?","choices":["Calcium blockers and beta blockers","Opioids such as codeine and morphine","Anticoagulants such as warfarin","Sodium channel blockers such as lidocaine"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Calcium blockers and beta blockers are listed to avoid with digoxin; they add to its slowing of atrioventricular conduction. — Antiarrhythmics.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"Which central nervous system effects are listed for digoxin toxicity?","choices":["Neuropathy and ataxia","Tinnitus and blurred vision","Confusion and visual disturbances","Nystagmus and muscle twitches"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Digoxin's central nervous system toxicity includes confusion, delirium, headache, seizures and visual disturbances. — Antiarrhythmics.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"Which antiarrhythmic may stop the heart for 2 to 3 seconds?","choices":["Esmolol","Digoxin","Adenosine","Magnesium sulfate"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Adenosine decreases conduction velocity and may stop the heart for 2 to 3 seconds; its half-life is about 10 seconds. — Antiarrhythmics.pptx, Slide 41"}]};
